hadiths[55]{hadithnumber,text}:
"370","Narrated Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that when the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to gather for prayer by estimating the time, and at that time, there was no formal call to prayer (adhan). One day, the Muslims discussed this matter (that there should be some announcement). Some suggested making a bell like the Christians’ bell, and some said no, rather make a horn like the Jews’ horn. Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, why not appoint a man who would call to prayer (give the adhan) for the prayer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Bilal, get up and give the adhan for the prayer.”"
"371","`Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) was commanded to say the Adhan in even (phrases) and the Iqamah in odd, except for ""Qad qamatis-salah""."
"372","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the call to prayer (adhan) is given, Satan turns his back and runs away (and out of fear) he passes wind as he runs (and keeps running) until he does not hear the sound of the adhan. Then when the adhan is completed, he comes back, and when the iqamah for prayer is pronounced, he turns his back and runs away again until the iqamah is completed. Then he comes back and puts whispers between a man and his heart. He says, 'Remember such and such, remember such and such,' things which he had not remembered before, until a person forgets how much he has prayed."""
"373","Narrated Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""When you call the Adhan, raise your voice, for whoever hears the voice of the Mu'adhdhin, whether jinn, human, or anything else, will testify for him on the Day of Resurrection."""
"374","Narrated by Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When he (peace be upon him) fought with any people along with us, he would not allow us to plunder until morning came and he (peace be upon him) would wait. If he heard the call to prayer, he would refrain from (killing) those people, and if he did not hear the call to prayer, he would attack them."
"375","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa‘id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you hear the call to prayer (adhan), then say as the muezzin says."""
"376","`Sayyiduna Amir Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him)` responded to the adhan up to ""Ashhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah"" just as the adhan is said, and when (the muezzin said) ""Hayya 'ala-s-Salah,"" he said ""La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah,"" and said that I heard the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it in this manner."
"377","Narrated from Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever hears the call to prayer (Adhan), then recites this supplication: 'O Allah! Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer! Grant Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) the means and virtue, and bestow upon him the praised station which You have promised him,' then my intercession will be permitted for him on the Day of Resurrection."""
"378","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If people knew what (reward) is in the call to prayer and in the first row, and they could not get it except by drawing lots, they would certainly draw lots. And if they knew what (reward) is in offering the Zuhr prayer at its earliest time, they would surely hasten to it. And if they knew what (reward) is in the Isha and Fajr prayers (offered in congregation), they would certainly come to them even if they had to crawl."""
"379","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him) gives the call to prayer."" Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) says that Sayyiduna Ibn Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him) was a blind man; he would not give the call to prayer until people said, 'Morning has come, morning has come.'"""
"380","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (had the habit that) when the muezzin would stand to give the call to prayer for Fajr and the Fajr adhan would be given, he would offer two light rak'ahs before the iqamah (takbeer) for the obligatory prayer."
"381","Sayyiduna Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""None of you should be prevented from (eating) sahur by Bilal’s (may Allah be pleased with him) call to prayer, because he gives the adhan at night so that the one among you who is praying tahajjud may return (for rest), and so that the one who is sleeping may wake up. And it is not that anyone should say that morning (has come)."" And the Prophet (peace be upon him) gestured with his fingers, raising them upwards and then lowering them down, until (he showed) like this (i.e., when the whiteness spreads). (The narrator Zuhair) placed his two index fingers one over the other, then spread them to his right and left (i.e., when the whiteness spreads in all directions), then understand that morning has come."
"382","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mughaffal Muzani (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Between every two calls to prayer, there is a prayer."" He said this three times (then said): ""If anyone wishes to offer it."" In another narration, it is stated that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Between every two calls to prayer (i.e., the adhan and the iqamah) there is a prayer, between every two calls to prayer (i.e., the adhan and the iqamah) there is a prayer."" Then, the third time, he said: ""If anyone wishes (he may offer it)."""
"383","Sayyiduna Malik bin Huwairith (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We came to the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and stayed in his company for twenty nights and days, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was gentle-hearted and kind. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) realized that we were longing to return to our families, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Return and stay among your people, teach them, command them (to do good), and establish prayer. When the time for prayer comes, let one of you give the call to prayer (adhan), and let the eldest among you lead the prayer."""
"384","Sayyiduna Malik bin Huwairith (may Allah be pleased with him) says that two persons came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the intention of traveling, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: ""When you set out (and the time for prayer comes), then give the adhan, then say the iqamah, and after that, the elder among you should lead you in prayer."""
"385","Narrated by Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to instruct the mu’adhdhin, after the adhan, to say: ""Pray in your places"" (and do not come for the congregation), and this was done on cold or rainy nights (when) on a journey."
"386","‘Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) says that while we were praying with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he heard the voices of some people. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he said: “What is the matter with you? (i.e., why was there this commotion?)” They replied that they had hurried for the prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Do not do so. When you come for the prayer, come with calmness and tranquility. Then whatever part of the prayer you catch, perform it, and whatever you miss, complete it.”"
"387","Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the Iqamah for prayer is called, do not stand up until you see me."""
"388","Narrated Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): Once the Iqamah for prayer was pronounced and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was talking quietly to a man in a corner of the mosque. He did not stand up for the prayer until some people started dozing off."
"389","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): (On one occasion) the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I have indeed intended to order that firewood be collected, then order for the call to prayer to be given for the prayer, then appoint a man to lead the people in prayer, and I would go to some people and burn their houses over them. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, if any one of them knew that he would find a fat bone or two good pieces of meat, he would surely come to the 'Isha prayer."""
"390","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The prayer in congregation is twenty-seven degrees superior (in reward) to the prayer offered alone."""
"391","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""The prayer in congregation is twenty-five times superior (in reward) to the prayer of anyone of you alone. And the angels of the night and the angels of the day gather at the Fajr prayer."" After that, Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: If you wish, recite this verse: ""Indeed, the recitation of the Qur'an at dawn is ever witnessed (i.e., attended by the angels)."" (Bani Isra'il: 78)"
"392","Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Among all people, those who will have the greatest reward for prayer are those whose distance (from the mosque) is the farthest, then those who are farther than them. And the person who waits for the prayer until he prays it with the Imam, in terms of reward, is greater than the one who prays quickly and goes to sleep."""
"393","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""A man was walking on a path when he saw a branch of thorns lying on the road, so he removed it. Allah Almighty rewarded him for this by forgiving him."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There are five types of martyrs: the one who dies of plague, the one who dies of a stomach illness, the one who drowns, the one who is crushed to death, and the one who is martyred in the way of Allah."" The rest of the hadith has already passed (see the Book: The Statement of Adhan... Chapter: Drawing lots regarding Adhan...)."
"394","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Banu Salamah wanted to leave their houses and settle somewhere near the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), but the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) disliked the idea of making Madinah deserted. So he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Banu Salamah! Do you not consider there to be reward in your footsteps (when you walk to the mosque)?"""
"395","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No prayer is heavier upon the hypocrites than the Fajr and Isha prayers, and if they knew what (reward) lies in them (offering these prayers at their proper times), they would surely come to them even if they had to crawl on their knees."""
"396","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""There are seven types of people whom Allah will grant shade under His shade on the Day when there will be no shade except His shade: (1) a just ruler, (2) a youth who grows up in the worship of his Lord, (3) a person whose heart is attached to the mosques, (4) two persons who love each other for the sake of Allah, meeting for His sake and parting for His sake, (5) a man whom a woman of status and beauty invites (to commit adultery) but he says, 'I fear Allah (so I cannot come),' (6) a person who gives charity so secretly that his left hand does not know what his right hand spends, (7) and a person who remembers Allah in solitude and his eyes become filled with tears."""
"397","Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Whoever goes to the mosque morning and evening, Allah will prepare for him a hospitality in Paradise every time he goes morning and evening."""
"398","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Malik bin Buhaina (may Allah be pleased with him), who belonged to the tribe of Azd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) saw a man performing two (2) rak‘ahs of prayer after the iqamah had been pronounced. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) finished (the prayer), the people gathered around that man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: ""Are there four rak‘ahs in the morning prayer? Are there four rak‘ahs in the morning prayer?"""
"399","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that when the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) was afflicted with the illness in which he passed away, and the time for prayer came and the call to prayer was given, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: “Tell Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer.” It was submitted to him (Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, herself submitted) that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) is a soft-hearted man, so when he stands in your place, (due to the intensity of grief) he will not be able to lead the prayer. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said again, but the same was submitted again. The third time, the Prophet (peace be upon him) again gave the command and said: “You are like the women who were companions of Yusuf (peace be upon him). Tell Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer.” So (it was conveyed and) Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) went to lead the prayer. Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) felt some relief in himself, so he came out supported by two men, as if I (even now) am looking at the Prophet’s (peace be upon him) feet, that due to the weakness of illness, they were dragging on the ground. So, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wanted to step back, but the Prophet (peace be upon him) gestured to him to remain in his place. Then the Prophet (peace be upon him) was brought until he sat beside Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). The Prophet (peace be upon him) would pray, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) would follow the Prophet’s (peace be upon him) prayer, and the people would follow Abu Bakr’s (may Allah be pleased with him) prayer. In one narration, it is mentioned that the Prophet (peace be upon him) sat on the left side of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and led the prayer."
"400","Umm al-Mu'minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says in another narration that when the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) fell ill and his illness intensified, he sought permission from his wives to be nursed in her house, so all of them granted permission.... The rest is the same as mentioned previously (see Book: The Statement of Adhan... Chapter: Up to what extent of illness should a sick person attend the congregation?)."
"401","`Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both)` once delivered the Friday sermon on a rainy day, and when the muezzin reached ""Hayya 'ala-s-Salah,"" he instructed him to say, ""As-salatu fi ar-rihal"" (Perform the prayer at your places). So the people began to look at each other, as if they disliked it. Then Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said, ""It seems that you have disliked this. Indeed, this was done by one who is better than me, meaning the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Indeed, Jumu'ah is obligatory, but I did not like that I should put you in hardship (that you should come treading mud up to your knees)."""
"402","Narrated Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): A man from the Ansar said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""I am unable (due to excuse) to pray with you,"" and he was a stout man. So he prepared food for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and invited him to his house. He spread a mat for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and washed one of its corners. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak‘ahs on it. Meanwhile, a man from the family of Jarud asked Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), ""Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) use to pray the Duha prayer?"" Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) replied, ""I never saw him pray it except on that day."""
"403","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When food is placed before you, eat before performing the Maghrib prayer, and do not hasten to the prayer by leaving your food."""
"404","It was asked from Ummul Momineen Aisha Siddiqa Tayyiba Tahira (may Allah be pleased with her), ""What did the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) do in his house?"" She replied that he used to be engaged in the household chores, that is, in serving his family members. Then when the time for prayer would come, he (peace and blessings be upon him) would go for the prayer."
"405","`Sayyiduna Malik bin Huwairith (may Allah be pleased with him) said that` I pray before you, and my intention is not to pray, but rather to show you the way I saw the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying."
"406","Narrated from Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa Tayyiba Tahira (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Tell Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer.” This has already been mentioned (see the chapter: To what extent should a sick person attend the congregation?). And in this narration, she says that I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Indeed, when Sayyiduna Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) stands in your place, due to his weeping, he will not be able to make the people hear (his recitation), so please order Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer. Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) says that I said to Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her): You should request the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when Sayyiduna Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) stands in your place, due to his weeping, he will not be able to make the people hear (his recitation). So Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) said it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Wait! Indeed, you women are like the companions of Yusuf (Joseph) (peace be upon him). Order Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” So Umm al-Mu’minin Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her): I have never benefited from you."
"407","Narrated Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): Abu Bakr As-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) used to lead the people in prayer during the illness of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which he passed away. When it was Monday and the people were standing in rows for prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted the curtain of his chamber and looked at us while standing. At that time, his blessed face was as bright as a page of the Qur'an. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled cheerfully. We were so delighted that we wanted to become distracted from the prayer due to seeing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) stepped back to join the row, thinking that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was coming out for prayer. But the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to us to complete our prayer and then let the curtain fall. On that very day, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away."
"408","Narrated from Sayyiduna Sahl bin Sa’d Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to the people of Banu Amr bin Awf to make peace among them. Meanwhile, the time for prayer came, so the muezzin came to the Commander of the Faithful, Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), and said to him, “If you lead the people in prayer, I will pronounce the iqamah?” He said, “Yes.” So Siddiq Akbar (may Allah be pleased with him) began to lead the prayer. In the meantime, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived while the people were engaged in prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered (the rows) until he stood in the (first) row, and the people began clapping. Siddiq Akbar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not look around in his prayer, but when the people clapped more, he looked back and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) signaled to him to remain in his place, but Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) raised both his hands and, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded him, he thanked Allah. Then Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) stepped back until he joined the row, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved forward and led the prayer. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said, “O Abu Bakr! When I instructed you, why did you not remain standing?” Sayyiduna Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) replied, “It is not for the son of Abu Quhafah to lead the prayer in front of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him).” Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, “What is the reason that I saw you clapping so much? (Listen) When something happens to someone in prayer, he should say ‘Subhan Allah,’ for when he says ‘Subhan Allah,’ attention will be paid to him. And clapping is only for women.”"
"409","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa Tayyiba Tahira (may Allah be pleased with her) says: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became ill, he asked: “Have the people performed the prayer?” We replied, “No, O Messenger of Allah! They are waiting for you.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Place water for me in a basin (so that I may bathe).” Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says: We did as he asked. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed, then tried to stand but fell unconscious. When he regained consciousness, he again asked: “Have the people performed the prayer?” We replied, “No, O Messenger of Allah! They are waiting for you.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Place water for me in a basin.” (So it was placed.) The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed, then tried to stand but fell unconscious. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Have the people performed the prayer?” We replied, “No, O Messenger of Allah! They are waiting for you.” The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Place water for me in a basin.” So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) got up and bathed, then tried to stand but fell unconscious. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Have the people performed the prayer?” We replied, “No, O Messenger of Allah! They are waiting for you, and the people are staying in the mosque waiting for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the ‘Isha prayer.” Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent (word) to Sayyiduna Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer. So the messenger reached him and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you to lead the people in prayer. Sayyiduna Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), who was a soft-hearted man, said: O Umar! You lead the people in prayer. Sayyiduna Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: You are more deserving of it. Then Sayyiduna Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) led the prayers during those remaining days. The rest of the hadith has already been mentioned above (see the chapter: To what extent should a sick person attend the congregation?)."
"410","The hadith of Umm al-Mu'minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed in his house during his illness has already been mentioned, and in this narration she says that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the imam prays sitting, then you should also pray sitting."" (But this command was later abrogated. See the chapter: To what extent should a sick person attend the congregation?)"
"411","Narrated by Sayyiduna Bara' (bin Azib, may Allah be pleased with him): When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said ""Sami' Allahu liman hamidah,"" none of us would bend his back (for prostration) until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went into prostration. After him, we would then go into prostration."
"412","Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""Does not any one of you who raises his head before the Imam fear that Allah may turn his head into the head of a donkey or his appearance into the appearance of a donkey?"""
"413","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""Listen! Obey! Even if an Abyssinian (Ethiopian) is appointed as your ruler, whose head is like a dried raisin."""
"414","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""These people who lead you in prayer, if they lead correctly, then there is reward for you, and if they make a mistake, then there is still reward for you and the sin is upon them."""
"415","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that one night he stayed at the house of his maternal aunt, Umm al-Mu’minin Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), as has been mentioned earlier (see: Book of Ablution... Chapter: Reciting the Qur’an after breaking ablution (without performing ablution)...). And in this narration, he says: “Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) slept until the sound of his breathing could be heard. After that, the mu’adhdhin came to him, and he (peace and blessings be upon him) went out and performed the Fajr prayer and did not perform ablution.” (In this hadith, there is mention of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) standing on the right side of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and praying with him.)"
"416","Narrated from Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sayyiduna Mu'adh bin Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) used to pray (the Isha) prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and then would return and lead his own people in prayer. (On one occasion) he led the Isha prayer and recited Surah Al-Baqarah. A man (broke his prayer and) left, which greatly upset Sayyiduna Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him). This news reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said to Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) three times: ""Fattan, Fattan, Fattan"" (one who causes trouble), or he said: ""Faatin, Faatin, Faatin"" (one who creates discord). And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) to recite two short surahs from the Mufassal."
"417","Sayyiduna Abu Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with him) says that a man said, “O Messenger of Allah! By Allah, I stay away from the morning prayer only because of so-and-so, as he makes the prayer too long for us.” Then I have never seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) more angry in giving advice than he was that day. After that, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Some of you make people dislike (worship). So whoever among you leads the people in prayer should make it short, for among the congregation are the weak, the elderly, and those with needs.”"
"418","`Sayyiduna Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him)` (the hadith which has passed above, see the chapter: When the Imam prolongs the prayer and a person has some need and he leaves (breaking the prayer) and offers the prayer elsewhere) says that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Why did you not lead the prayer with (Sabbi hisma rabbikal a'la) and (Wash-shamsi wa duhaha) and (Wallaili idha yaghsha)?"""
"419","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) says that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform a short prayer and would perform it perfectly (completely)."
"420","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: When I stand for prayer, I intend to prolong it, but on hearing the crying of a child, I shorten my prayer, disliking that I might cause hardship to his mother."
"421","Narrated Nu'man bin Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Straighten your rows, or Allah will turn your faces."""
"422","Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Straighten your rows and stand close together, for I can see you from behind my back."""
"423","Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to offer the Tahajjud prayer in his chamber, and the wall of the chamber was low, so people saw the body of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and some people stood up to follow him in prayer. When morning came, they talked about it. Then on the second night, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) stood up, and some people again stood up to follow him in prayer that night as well. For two or three nights, people did the same, until when the next night came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stayed at home and did not come out to pray. In the morning, people mentioned this, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “I feared that (due to this regularity) the prayer (Tahajjud) might be made obligatory upon you.”"
"424","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) in this hadith (in addition to the previous hadith) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (came in the morning and) said: ""I have understood what I saw of your action (that is, you are eager for worship), so O people! Pray in your homes, for the best prayer of a person among his prayers is the one he performs in his house, except for the obligatory prayer."""
